# `M154c` / `C5` — the known-answer plant for `after`, in both of its scopes.
#
# **This file is meant to fail.** The second test below ends red on purpose, and that is the
# assertion: `tflw spec` says this construct "runs whether the test passed or failed", and nothing
# in this repository has ever checked the second half of that sentence.
#
# More than that — nothing here has ever been able to check the *first* half either.
# `tests/examples/hooks-explained.tflw` has an `after` hook that deletes what its test created. If
# that hook simply never ran, **nothing in that file fails**: the test already passed, the cleanup
# is invisible to it, and the suite stays green. It is a construct exercised in a way that cannot go
# red in the direction that matters, which is `D722`'s bar stated as a defect.
#
# So the hooks here do not clean anything up. They mark a counter, and the counter is the claim:
#
#   `c5-after-file` == 1   `after file` runs ONCE for the file, not once per test
#   `c5-after-test` == 2   `after` runs once per test — INCLUDING the one that failed
#
# Three distinct defects are separated by those two integers. A hook that never runs leaves 0. A
# hook that skips failed tests leaves 1. An `after file` implemented at test scope leaves 2.

before file
  api POST /lifecycle/reset
  expect status equals 200

after file
  # Deliberately not cleanup. A side effect whose absence is measurable is the only kind of hook
  # body that can make this construct fail.
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c5-after-file" }
  expect status equals 200

after
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c5-after-test" }
  expect status equals 200

@constructs @lifecycle
test "the passing test — its `after` must run"
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200

@constructs @lifecycle
test "the failing test — its `after` must run too, and this is the half nothing checked"
  api GET /health
  # Deliberately false. `/health` answers 200; 599 is not a status this app can return.
  expect status equals 599
